<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wu Zetian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/690">690</a>–<a href="/years/705">705</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Empress Wu Zetian · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wu Zetian was the only woman to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as emperor in her own right, reigning from 690 to 705. A consort of the Tang emperors Taizong and Gaozong, she wielded power as empress and regent before proclaiming her own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>. Her reign was marked by the promotion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a>, and the Tang was restored after her abdication.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wu-zetian.jpg" alt="Wu Zetian" width="237" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Likely to be Zhang Xuan (张萱) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%94%90%E5%90%8E%E8%A1%8C%E5%BE%9E%E5%9C%96_(%E5%B1%80%E9%83%A8)_-_%E6%AD%A6%E5%89%87%E5%A4%A9.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wu Zetian was born in 624, and her personal name was Wu Zhao. Her birthplace is not documented, and scholars place it variously at Wenshui, at Lizhou in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, or at the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. She was first a concubine of Emperor Taizong, who gave her the art name Wu Mei, meaning glamorous. By early 650 she was a concubine of Emperor Gaozong, with the title Zhaoyi, the highest ranking of the nine concubines of the second rank. Gaozong was inexperienced and often incapacitated by dizzy spells, and he was named heir only because of the disgrace of his two older brothers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 655 Wu became Gaozong's empress consort, and in 656 the heir apparent Li Zhong was deposed in favour of her son Li Hong. In 657 she and her allies took reprisals against officials who had opposed her elevation: several chancellors were sent to remote prefectures, and in 659 Zhangsun Wuji was exiled and forced to commit suicide. After Gaozong suffered a debilitating stroke in 660 she effectively administered the empire for him until his death in 683, and she then became empress dowager and regent. Her third son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhongzong-of-tang">Li Zhe</a>, took the throne in 684 as Zhongzong for about six weeks.</p><p>In 690 she had Emperor Ruizong yield the throne to her, proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> and became emperor, and she is remembered as the only undisputed female sovereign in Chinese history. Her de facto rule lasted 45 years. She institutionalised the use of informants and enforced stringent legal measures, purged members of the Tang royal house and veteran officials of earlier administrations, and reformed the civil service to promote merit. She patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, literature and the arts and campaigned against Turkic and Tibetan incursions. Her rule formed part of the High Tang period, a peak in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s political power and cultural influence, though her later governance became increasingly autocratic and extravagant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wu was removed in the Shenlong Coup, which abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> and restored the Tang, and she died on 16 December 705, a few months later. Traditional historians believed that she had killed her own children, but these rumours seem to have surfaced 400 years after her death and probably owed much to the belief that a woman was unfit to hold imperial power. Chinese writers call her Wu Hou as empress, and Wu Zetian, her posthumous title, is the name by which she is generally known.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wu Zetian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Empress Wu Zetian</b> (r. 690–705) interrupted the Tang line by proclaiming her own Zhou dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Wu Zetian</b>, the only woman to rule China as emperor in her own right, gave up the throne, and her self-styled Zhou dynasty was abolished in favour of the restored Tang.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-23">23 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Wu Zetian</b>, the only woman to rule China as emperor in her own right, was interred in a joint burial with her husband, the Tang emperor Gaozong, in the Qianling Mausoleum, where he had been buried in 684.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-2">2 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Wu Zetian</b>, long the power behind the Tang throne, took the title of emperor and founded her own Zhou dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-16">16 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Wu Zhao</b>, a concubine of Emperor Taizong, became empress to Emperor Gaozong in 655 and gradually took over government as he fell ill.</blockquote><a href="/state/wu-zhou">Wu Zhou</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Guangyuan, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.44,105.84&amp;year=697" aria-label="Guangyuan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7268 984 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wu Zetian"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7718 1254h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7718 1254h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.44,105.84&amp;year=697">What was here in 697 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
